            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent Pelvic Infections and Salpingitis after Perforated Appendicitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e177-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2015.04.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-3188(15)00171-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The effect of perforated appendicitis on the adnexa is an issue of concern and controversy. Long-term fertility studies have been conflicting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We present the case of a patient with chronic pelvic infections, salpingitis, and hydrosalpinx after perforated appendicitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Magnetic resonance imaging was helpful in diagnosing a chronically obstructed fallopian tube, likely secondary to the dense adhesions from her previously treated perforated appendicitis. Salpingectomy relieved her symptoms of chronic pain and recurrent infections.</AbstractText>
